# A line comment, running to the end of the line.

##
    A block comment. It opens with two marks and closes with the next two, and everything on
    the closing line goes with them.

    That last part settles two questions at once. There is no nesting to get wrong, because
    there is no depth to count: the first pair after the opener closes it, whatever was
    written in between. And nothing can follow a closer and still be code, so a comment is a
    line of its own or the end of a line, never a parenthesis in the middle of one.

    A block may span as many lines as it needs. Its text is indented under the marks, which
    is a convention rather than a rule: the compiler reads a block the same either way, and
    an editor folds a long one away by indentation alone.
##

## A block may open and close on one line. ##

#########################################################################
#  A run of marks is a heading rather than a mistake, since the extra   #
#  ones are simply part of the comment.                                 #
#########################################################################

shared model Comments

    # A comment may sit on its own line inside a model,

    constant integer Answer = 42;      # or trail a declaration on the same line.

    function Placement()
        # A comment before a statement.
        integer total = 0;

        total = total + 1;             # A comment after one.

        ##
            A block comment inside a function body, taking the indentation of the code around
            it. Nothing written in here can close it early except another pair of marks, and a
            single mark cannot.
        ##

        if total > 0
            # Comments sit inside any construct.
            total = total - 1;
        end if
    end function

end model

# A trailing comment at the very end of the file.
